---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-chronic-kidney-stones`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D and Kidney Stones',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Kidney Stones', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and Kidney Stones: When Supplementation Is Safe and When to Worry"
  description="Vitamin D and kidney stones — the calcium-oxalate stone paradox, VITAL and WHI-CaD data, dietary vs supplemental calcium, and safe dosing in stone-formers."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-chronic-kidney-stones", label: "Kidney Stones" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and Kidney Stones</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Kidney stones (predominantly calcium oxalate) affect ~10% of adults over
      a lifetime. Vitamin D's role is nuanced — routine supplementation
      probably doesn't increase stone risk, but combining vitamin D with
      high-dose calcium supplements does. Dietary calcium (paradoxically)
      protects. Stone-formers can safely take vitamin D with attention to
      dosing and calcium source.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">The trial data</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>VITAL (2019)</strong> — 25,000 adults; 2,000 IU/day vitamin D: no significant increase in kidney stones vs placebo over 5 years.</li>
      <li><strong>WHI-CaD (2006)</strong> — 36,000 women; 400 IU vitamin D + 1,000 mg calcium: 17% increase in kidney stones vs placebo. The signal traced to the calcium supplement, not vitamin D per se.</li>
      <li><strong>D-Health (2023)</strong> — 21,000 adults; 60,000 IU monthly: no increase in urolithiasis.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Dietary vs supplemental calcium</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Counterintuitively, higher dietary calcium REDUCES kidney stone risk
      (Curhan 1993, 1997). Dietary calcium binds oxalate in the gut,
      preventing absorption and urinary excretion. Supplemental calcium taken
      away from meals doesn't bind oxalate and slightly increases stone risk.
      Take calcium supplements WITH meals if you're a stone-former.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Safe vitamin D dosing in stone-formers</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>Vitamin D 1,000-2,000 IU/day is generally safe.</li>
      <li>If calcium supplement is needed, keep dose ≤ 500 mg per dose and take with meals.</li>
      <li>Prefer dietary calcium (dairy, fortified foods) over supplements.</li>
      <li>Hydration matters more than dosing tweaks — aim for &gt; 2 L urine output daily.</li>
      <li>Reduce dietary oxalate (spinach, rhubarb, nuts, chocolate, tea) if you form calcium-oxalate stones.</li>
      <li>Reduce sodium and animal protein — both raise urinary calcium.</li>
      <li>Consider 24-hour urine collection to characterise stone risk profile.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Absorptive vs resorptive hypercalciuria</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Some stone-formers have primary intestinal hyperabsorption of calcium
      (type I absorptive hypercalciuria) — these patients are more sensitive
      to vitamin D-driven calcium changes. Others have renal calcium leak or
      resorptive causes. A workup by a stone specialist can identify the
      dominant mechanism, which changes management.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Practical guide</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      For most kidney-stone patients: vitamin D 1,000-2,000 IU/day is safe,
      dietary calcium 1,000 mg/day with meals is protective, adequate
      hydration is the single most important intervention. Recurrent stone-
      formers benefit from urologic workup and, in some cases, thiazide
      diuretics or potassium citrate.
    </p>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Acute severe flank pain, blood in
      urine, or fever with UTI symptoms requires urgent medical attention —
      infected obstructing stones are surgical emergencies.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-chronic-kidney-stones" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
